{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent low bias: model forecasts are systematically below realized prices by ~+1 to +3 EUR for most of the first ~20 forecast days during rising regimes; apply an upward level shift when the last 10–15 days show a positive drift.",
    "Trend/acceleration underestimation: when spot breaks upward (multi-day rally), the model follows but with insufficient slope; add a momentum term (e.g., boost forecast slope) for horizons ~t+3 to t+15.",
    "Late-horizon reversal error: beyond ~t+20 the model often projects continued strength (or even accelerates) while outcomes flatten or mean-revert; dampen trend and pull forecasts toward a medium-term mean for t+20 to t+30.",
    "Overshoot after spikes: the model can overshoot highs around days ~25–30 (e.g., projecting 70–72 when realized stays ~66–69); cap late-horizon upside extensions after a recent spike and increase mean reversion strength.",
    "Volatility smoothing: the model’s day-to-day swings are too muted early on (misses sharp up-moves); widen response to recent volatility for short horizons (t+1 to t+10) but simultaneously enforce stronger reversion for long horizons."
  ]
}
